import AtmosphereNav from '../../../components/AtmosphereNav.astro';
---

<Layout>
	<Breadcrumb crumbs={[
		{ label: 'Home', href: '/' },
		{ label: 'Calculators', href: '/calculators' },
		{ label: 'U.S. Standard Atmosphere Calculator' },
	]} />
	<h1>U.S. Standard Atmosphere Calculator</h1>
	<p>Tabulate atmospheric properties as a function of altitude using the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Standard_Atmosphere" target="_blank">U.S. Standard Atmosphere 1976 model</a>.</p>
	<AtmosphereNav active="notes" />

	<h2>About the U.S. Standard Atmosphere</h2>
	<p>
	The U.S. Standard Atmosphere models how atmospheric properties vary with altitude from -5 to 1000 km. 
	Formulas are derived from the file "ussa1976.dpr" in <a href="https://www.pdas.com/atmosdownload.html" target="_blank">the 1976 U.S. Standard Atmosphere of 1976</a>, which
	covers the lower atmosphere from -5 to 86 km, and the upper atmosphere from 86 to 1000 km.
	The boiling point of water is calculated using <a href="https://www.iapws.org/relguide/IF97-Rev.html" target="_blank">IAPWS-95</a> and is accurate to 
	0.01°C (0.02°F). At pressures below water's triple point (611.657 Pa), the boiling point is undefined because liquid water cannot exist.
	</p>
	<p>
	Note that density and speed of sound differ from the U.S. Standard Atmosphere by about 0.002%, as this calculator uses the value of the gas constant fixed in the 2019 SI revision. Density is computed for dry air; humid air can be up to 2% less dense than dry air. Density altitude and pressure 
	altitude are the altitudes at which air density and pressure (respectively) equal the standard atmosphere at that altitude.
	</p>
	<h2>Standard sea-level conditions</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Pressure: 1 atm (101325 Pa, 1013.25 hPa, 760 mmHg, 14.6959 psi, 29.9213 inHg)</li>
		<li>Temperature: 15°C (59°F, 288.15 K, 518.67°R)</li>
		<li>Lapse rate: -6.5°C/km (0.00357°F/ft) under 11 km, varies above 11 km</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Changes with altitude</h2>
	<p>
	In the troposphere (under 11 km), temperature decreases with altitude by about 6.5°C/km (0.00357°F/ft), a quantity known as the lapse rate, and 
	pressure decreases roughly exponentially, by about 50% per 5500 m (18000 ft) of altitude. In the stratosphere (11-51 km), temperatures stay stable 
	and then increase with altitude. In the mesosphere (51-86 km), temperatures decrease with altitude again, and in the thermosphere (86-1000 km), 
	temperatures again increase with altitude.
	</p>
	<p>
	When reporting pressure at sea level, two conventions are used: sea-level pressure and altimeter setting. Sea-level pressure extrapolates the 
	observed air pressure to sea level using the standard lapse rate and the hydrostatic equation. Because a warmer atmosphere is less dense 
	and has a greater scale height, higher surface temperatures yield higher sea-level pressures for a given reading. Altimeter setting uses the same 
	extrapolation but fixes the sea-level temperature at 15°C (59°F), making it independent of actual conditions. As a result, altimeter setting tends 
	to exceed sea-level pressure in summer and fall below it in winter.
	</p>
	<p>
	Example: At the summit of Mount Everest (8849 m, 29032 ft), typical summer conditions 
	are <a href="https://rmets.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/wea.3931" target="_blank">-19°C (-2°F)</a> and <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10066724/" target="_blank">336 hPa (0.332 atm)</a>.
	If the standard lapse rate extended down to sea level, sea-level pressure would be 980 hPa and sea-level temperature would be 37.4°C (99.3°F),
	well above the standard 15°C (59°F). If sea-level temperature were 15°C (59°F) and the same standard lapse rates applied, with the same summit pressure
	of 336 hPa, the pressure at sea level would instead be 1081 hPa, which is the altimeter setting.
	</p>
	<h2>Geometric versus geopotential altitude</h2>
	<p>
	Geometric altitude is the actual height above mean sea level, while geopotential altitude is a "corrected" altitude representing the altitude in a
	uniform gravitational field of 9.80665 m/s<sup>2</sup> that would require the same work to reach as the actual altitude in the real gravity field. 
	Gravity weakens slightly at higher altitudes, due to the greater distance from earth's center, so at very high altitudes geopotential altitude is 
	significantly less than geometric altitude.
	</p>
</Layout>
